---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/foundational/from-data-to-decisions">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Data Science, and it assumes nothing — no programming, no
    statistics beyond an average, no spreadsheet wizardry. If you have ever looked at a chart
    and wondered whether it really proves what the headline says it proves, you have already
    started.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what data science actually is — a workflow that turns questions into decisions — and name the three skills it stands on;</li>
    <li>tell categorical data from numerical data, and why the difference decides how data can be summarised and shown;</li>
    <li>see a real relationship through measurement noise, and know when to stop fitting;</li>
    <li>walk the six-step workflow — question, collect, clean, explore, model and validate, communicate — and name the trap each step hides;</li>
    <li>explain selection bias, overfitting and the correlation-causation gap in plain language, and apply the professional defences against each.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit rules everything here: <em>the question comes first</em>. Data never speaks for
    itself; it answers whatever you ask, and it answers vague questions vaguely. Every section
    below is the same habit applied at a different scale.
  </p>

  <h2>A first-grade chart is already data science</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the smallest data analysis in the world. A teacher asks a class of five-year-olds
    their favourite pet and tallies the answers: dogs 12, cats 8, hamsters 3. Notice what the
    teacher does next — not "look at the numbers" but a question: <em>what does the data tell
    us, and does it answer our question?</em> Twelve dogs beat eight cats by four; twenty-three
    children answered in all; hamsters came last. The chart was never the point. The point was
    a decision the numbers could inform — which pet book to buy for the classroom shelf, say.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="rebuild the smallest analysis in the world" widget="order" config={dsFlowOrder} />
  <p>
    That little exercise already contains the whole discipline in miniature. A question came
    first. Data was collected to answer it. The tallies were organised into a form a human can
    read, then interpreted against the question. Data analysis, in the classic definition, is
    exactly this: a body of methods that help describe facts, detect patterns, develop
    explanations and test hypotheses — used in every science, in business, in administration
    and in policy. What data science adds is scale and machinery: the same loop, run on
    millions of records, by people who combine enough <em>statistics</em> to reason about
    uncertainty, enough <em>computing</em> to work with data too large for a spreadsheet, and
    enough <em>domain knowledge</em> to know which questions are worth asking and what the
    numbers actually measure. Take any one leg away and the work stops being science: without
    statistics it is dashboard decoration; without computing it cannot scale; without domain
    knowledge it produces precise answers to pointless questions.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pair each leg with what its absence costs" widget="match" config={dsLegsMatch} />
  <p>
    The unglamorous truth about the profession is that most of the craft is plumbing and
    honesty: asking sharp questions, cleaning messy evidence, plotting before modelling,
    communicating uncertainty without deflating it into noise. Every real project — a hospital
    deciding which patients need follow-up calls, a streaming service deciding which shows to
    renew — walks the same steps, usually more than once, because good analysis loops back
    when the data answers a better question than the one you asked.
  </p>

  <h2>Two kinds of data, and why the kind matters</h2>
  <p>
    Every dataset is built from two kinds of entries. <em>Categorical</em> data places each
    observation into a labelled group — favourite pet, plan type, day of the week; each value
    belongs to exactly one category. <em>Numerical</em> (or measurement) data records a
    quantity — viewing hours, height, age, a count of books. The distinction sounds small and
    is not: the kind of data decides what summaries make sense (an "average pet" is
    nonsense; the <em>most common</em> pet is the right summary for categories) and which
    charts are honest (bar charts for categories, scatter plots and histograms for
    quantities). Most real datasets mix both, and the first minutes with any new file are
    spent deciding, column by column, which kind each variable is — because the kind decides
    what questions the column can answer.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="file each summary under the kind of data it serves" widget="sort-buckets" config={dsKindSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> data science is the child's tally chart grown up — question,
    collect, organise, interpret — at industrial scale, with three legs under it. Now for the
    skill that separates analysis from wishful thinking: reading a real pattern out of noisy
    data.
  </p>

  <h2>Seeing the line through the noise</h2>
  <p>
    Real data never sits exactly on the pattern it follows. Measure anything twice and the
    answers differ a little; people, sensors and systems are imperfect. So the first visual
    skill of data science is seeing the simple relationship underneath the scatter — and the
    first honesty skill is admitting when there is no relationship at all.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="ten noisy observations, one straight answer — the line was worth fitting before anything fancier" widget="plotter" config={scatterCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> hover the dots — each one wanders off the blue line by
    measurement noise, yet the underlying relationship is exactly <code>y = 2x + 1</code>. The
    line was worth fitting before anything fancier, and it describes the pattern better than
    any single dot does. Now the reverse skill: with different data, those same ten points
    could be pure scatter, and the professional judgement is telling a real signal from
    wishful fitting. Demanding that data sit perfectly on a model leads to modelling the
    noise — a mistake with a name (overfitting) that you will meet again in step five.
  </p>

  <h2>The six-step workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Between "we have a question" and "here is your decision" lies a sequence of steps so
    stable it has held for decades. Learn it as a checklist now; the rest of the lesson
    walks it with the traps each step hides.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the six-step workflow" widget="equationStep" config={flowCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the steps are in a deliberate order, and two of them are
    not what a newcomer expects. Cleaning (step 3) is where practitioners say most of the
    project time goes — real data is collected by fallible systems for other purposes.
    Exploration (step 4) comes <em>before</em> modelling, inverting the classroom habit: look
    at the data until it tells you which model would be honest, rather than fitting a model
    and checking it against the data. And step 6 is part of the workflow, not an optional
    extra — a result that does not change a decision was entertainment.
  </p>

  <h2>Step by step, with the traps each step hides</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>1 · Question first.</strong> Every project starts with a decision someone needs
    to make: which pages lose subscribers, which patients will miss appointments, where
    should the next depot go. A well-formed question names its decision, its deadline and
    its acceptable error. The famous historical example is John Snow and cholera (1854): the
    question was not "what causes cholera?" — too broad — but "do deaths cluster around a
    particular water source?" — narrow enough that a map of fatalities around the Broad
    Street pump could <em>answer</em> it. Sharp questions make data decisive; vague
    questions make data decorative.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="sharpen the question — the Snow decision" widget="branch" config={dsSnowBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>2 · Collect.</strong> Data arrives from databases, sensors, surveys, logs — and
    the act of collection is itself a measuring instrument. If customer records only include
    people who finished signup, every conclusion silently describes finishers, not potential
    customers; if a survey only reaches people with landlines, it describes 1995. This is
    <strong>selection bias</strong>, and its signature is that the data never mentions what
    it is missing. The professional habit is to write down, before analysing, <em>how</em>
    each field was produced and who is absent from the file.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blanks — the bias that hides by omission" widget="cloze" config={dsBiasCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>3 · Clean.</strong> Names spelled three ways, dates in two formats, impossible
    ages (230), duplicated rows, missing values coded as 0, −1, blank or the string "NULL" —
    each is a small lie that propagates into every downstream number. The rules: never clean
    silently (document every transformation), never clean toward the answer you expect, and
    prefer fixing the pipeline over hand-editing the symptom. A model trained on wrongly
    joined data is confidently wrong forever.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the cleaning rule that got broken" widget="spot-error" config={dsCleanSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>4 · Explore.</strong> Plot first, model second. Histograms reveal skew;
    scatter plots reveal relationships; box plots reveal outliers; summary tables reveal
    impossible values. This is <strong>exploratory data analysis</strong>, formalised by
    John Tukey (1962), and its philosophy inverts the classroom: do not fit a model and
    check it against the data — look at the data until it tells you which model would be
    honest. The scatter above is exploration doing its job: before anyone proposes a neural
    network, ten dots and a straight line have already settled the first question.
    Exploration also calibrates scepticism: Anscombe's quartet (1973) shows four datasets
    with <em>identical</em> averages, spreads and fitted lines — and wildly different
    pictures. Plotting is not presentation; it is inspection.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="two habits at step four — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={dsEdaCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>5 · Model &amp; validate.</strong> Fit the simplest thing that could work —
    often a baseline ("predict the average", "predict yesterday's value") — then something
    slightly smarter, and only promote complexity that beats the baseline on data it has
    <em>not seen</em>. That last clause is the entire safeguard against the field's central
    disease, <strong>overfitting</strong>: a flexible model can memorise the noise in its
    training data, scoring beautifully there and embarrassingly on new data. The defence is
    to hold out a test set (or cross-validate) and report performance only there.
    Complexity must earn its keep on unseen data; a parameter that improves training fit
    but not test fit is a lie the model tells itself.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="bet before you read on — the diagnosis" widget="predict" config={dsOverfitPredict} />
  <p>
    <strong>6 · Communicate.</strong> A result that does not change a decision was
    entertainment. The deliverables are a number with its uncertainty ("renewal likely lifts
    retention by 2–4 percent, and we are 90 percent confident the effect is positive"), a
    chart honest enough to survive scrutiny (axes from zero, baselines shown), and a
    recommendation with its assumptions stated. Uncertainty is content, not weakness: a
    forecast delivered without a range invites decisions the evidence cannot support.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each deliverable under honest reporting or theatre" widget="sort-buckets" config={dsCommSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> six steps — question, collect, clean, explore, model and
    validate, communicate — each with a characteristic trap. Now watch all six carry one
    real decision from first question to shipped recommendation.
  </p>

  <h2>A case end to end: why do subscribers leave?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Question</strong>: a streaming service is losing subscribers; the finance team
    wants to know whether to spend a fixed retention budget on discounts or on content.
    Sharpened: <em>which members are likely to cancel next month, and is there a lever we
    control?</em> <strong>Collect</strong>: eighteen months of account records — signup
    date, plan, viewing hours per week, devices used, payment history, and the flag that
    matters: who actually cancelled. Note the instrument immediately: the file contains only
    people who <em>signed up</em> (selection: it says nothing about those who bounced off
    the landing page), and viewing hours are tracked only when the app is open (measurement:
    hours near zero could mean dissatisfaction <em>or</em> a holiday). <strong>Clean</strong>:
    duplicates from a billing-system migration are removed; "hours per week" for a fortnight
    with a two-week outage is recoded as missing rather than zero; the eleven accounts with
    negative tenure are dropped and the pipeline bug reported. Every transformation is
    logged.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Explore</strong>: the cancellations are not uniform — churn spikes in month
    three (the free-trial cliff), among members whose viewing fell by more than half
    month-over-month, and on the ad-supported plan. A scatter of viewing hours against
    tenure shows the falling-engagement pattern plainly, and a box plot shows churners'
    engagement dropping weeks <em>before</em> they cancel — the signal a retention offer
    could intercept. <strong>Model &amp; validate</strong>: the baseline ("everyone
    renews") is useless for targeting; a simple model — flag members with declining hours
    in their second month — is tested on the six months it never saw and catches members
    at several times the base rate of churn, which is the number that matters (you cannot
    save everyone; you need a list worth calling). A fancier model is tried and rejected:
    it beats the simple one on training data but not on held-out data — complexity did not
    earn its keep. <strong>Communicate</strong>: "Members whose viewing drops by half after
    month one cancel at several times the average rate; an email offering a free month to
    that group is projected to retain one in eight of them at a cost well below a month's
    subscription — we recommend a two-market trial with a control group, and we will know
    in six weeks whether to scale." Numbers, uncertainty, a controlled test, a decision.
    That is the workflow doing exactly what it was built for.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a model that is 95 percent accurate is a good model.
  Accuracy inherits the base rate: if five percent of members churn in a month, the lazy
  model "predict that everyone renews" scores 95 percent and saves nobody — which is
  exactly why the case above reports "catches members at several times the base rate"
  instead of a percentage correct. The metric must match the decision: how many of the
  people you can act on were caught, at what cost in false alarms — not raw accuracy.</p>

  <h2>What separates professionals</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the workflow, four habits mark the difference between analysis and theatre.
    <strong>Baselines first</strong> — a model that cannot beat "guess the average" is not a
    model, and running the dumb baseline first costs minutes and saves careers; a baseline
    is the point of reference that makes every later improvement measurable. <strong>The
    dataset is an instrument</strong> — ask what it measures, in what population, with what
    gaps, before asking what it says. <strong>Correlation still is not causation</strong> —
    ice-cream sales and drownings rise together because of summer, not dessert; dashboards
    make it easier than ever to imply causation by arrangement alone, and the honest tools
    (randomised experiments, natural experiments, careful adjustment) are deliberate acts,
    not side effects of a clever chart. The famous cautionary tale is Google Flu Trends
    (2008): it predicted flu from search terms brilliantly — then confidently
    over-predicted for years as its own recommendations changed what people searched, a
    lesson in how the instrument changes the thing measured. <strong>Reproducibility is
    trust</strong> — a result nobody can re-run from the raw data and code is an anecdote
    with charts; versioned code, recorded data lineage and saved random seeds are what make
    a finding an asset rather than a story.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the data will speak for itself." It is the field's most
  dangerous slogan. Data speaks only through the question asked, the way it was gathered and
  the model imposed on it — all human choices. Data science is also not mostly machine
  learning: the best practitioners spend most of their effort on steps 1–3 and 6, which is
  precisely where the judgement lives.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A colleague says: "we have three million rows of user data — let's explore it and see what we find." What is wrong with this as a project opener, and what should replace it?</li>
    <li>A city's fitness-app data shows its residents are the healthiest in the country. Name the bias, and say who is missing from the file.</li>
    <li>Why is "predict the average" run first, before any clever model?</li>
    <li>Ice-cream sales correlate with drownings. Does ice cream cause drowning? What is the honest third variable, and what kind of study would settle the question?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) "Explore the data" is not a question — the workflow starts
    with a decision someone needs to make, sharpened until data could answer it (step 1);
    exploring first wastes the one resource that never comes back and usually finds patterns
    that answer no decision at all. (2) Selection bias: the file measures people who
    <em>download a fitness app</em> — it is silent about everyone else, who may be healthy
    without tracking it or unable to afford the phone the app requires. (3) A baseline is the
    point of reference: without it you cannot tell whether a complex model's improvement is
    skill or self-deception, and a model that cannot beat the dumb baseline is not a model.
    (4) No — summer causes both (swimming, and heat that sells ice cream); a randomised or
    natural experiment that varies ice-cream availability while holding season fixed would
    settle it. Correlation plus a plausible confounder is a hypothesis, not a finding.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Data science is a workflow that turns questions into decisions, resting on statistics, computing and domain knowledge — remove any leg and it stops being science.</li>
    <li>The question comes first: sharp questions make data decisive; "explore and see what we find" makes it decorative.</li>
    <li>Categorical data takes labels and mode; numerical data takes quantities, averages and scatter plots — the kind decides the honest summary.</li>
    <li>Every dataset is an instrument: selection bias hides in who is missing, measurement bias in how a field was produced.</li>
    <li>Clean documented, explore before modelling, validate on unseen data, and report uncertainty as content.</li>
    <li>Baselines make improvement measurable; correlation is a hypothesis; reproducibility is what makes a finding an asset.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson named the whole workflow; the next lessons slow down where the time actually
    goes. <em>Cleaning messy data</em> takes step 3 apart — missing values, duplicates,
    joins gone wrong — because silent errors there poison every downstream number. Later
    come the modelling and ethics layers in depth. The habits you built here — question
    first, instrument awareness, validation on unseen data — are the ones every later lesson
    assumes.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three layers lie beneath the workflow. <strong>The experimental layer</strong>: the gold
    standard for turning correlation into causation is the randomised controlled trial —
    randomly assign the new feature to half the users, compare outcomes. Randomisation
    balances every confounder, known and unknown, which is why A/B testing is the workhorse
    of internet decision-making; when experiments are impossible (you cannot randomise
    smoking), statistics offers quasi-experimental tools — difference-in-differences,
    regression discontinuity — each with stated assumptions. <strong>The statistical
    layer</strong>: every estimate has a distribution, and aggregates can reverse at the
    subgroup level (Simpson's paradox — the Berkeley admissions case is the canonical
    example), so professional practice reports uncertainty intervals and interrogates
    subgroups before believing totals. <strong>The human layer</strong>: data about people
    is data about people — privacy, consent, and bias in, bias out. A hiring model trained
    on past hires learns the past's discriminations as signal; a credit model can encode
    geography as a proxy for race. This is why modern practice treats ethics as an
    engineering requirement — audits, fairness metrics, documented datasets — rather than a
    press-release afterthought.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a good A/B test simply runs until the results look
  significant, then ships. Optional stopping quietly breaks the error-rate guarantee: check
  often enough and pure noise will eventually cross the 5 percent line, so a "significant"
  peeked test can be mostly false alarm. The fix is pre-commitment — sample size or analysis
  window fixed in advance — the same discipline pre-registration imposes on clinical trials,
  and for the same reason.</p>
  <p>
    The future of the field is argued over — automated pipelines versus craftsman analysis —
    but the six steps have been stable for decades, precisely because they encode something
    machines do not supply by default: the discipline of being honest with yourself in
    public.
  </p>

